<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation of Role-Playing Agents</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the accuracy and vividness of Role-Playing Agents (RPAs), existing research generally focuses on three dimensions: knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, personality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, and emotional consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, where both knowledge and personality fall under the category of role consistency.
As shown in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ I Introduction ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, these evaluations of RPAs are based on the content of text responses, lacking assessments that semantically decouple multimodal emotional expressions.
Notably, dimensions regarding general capabilities, such as dialogue ability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and empathy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, are beyond the scope of this paper.
Furthermore, existing automatic modality-synthesis metrics, including audio (e.g., WER, CER, SS) and video (e.g., CPBD, SSIM, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sync_{cf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sync_{cf}</annotation></semantics></math>) metrics, primarily focus on low-level perceptual or signal fidelity, but fail to evaluate the underlying semantic-level properties such as cross-modal consistency, emotional expressiveness, and role-style consistency, thereby still relying primarily on human judgment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multimodal Role-Playing Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing role-playing datasets can be divided into two aspects: 
<span id="S2.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S2.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">textual role-playing datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>;
</span></span>
<span id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S2.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">multimodal role-playing datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
</span></span>
</span>
However, these datasets are primarily constructed by synthetic methods, lacking in-depth integration of real dynamic emotions. On the other hand, most early multimodal emotional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> or empathetic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> dialogue datasets lack role-related data and detailed semantic descriptions of multimodal data. These datasets fail to support semantically decoupled evaluation on EC and RC, thereby motivating the construction of proposed dataset <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span>. Detailed comparison can be found in Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ I Introduction ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21941v1/evaluation_framework.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/240;" width="476" height="240" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Framework of <span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY</span>. <span id="S2.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upper Left</span>: dialogue format of evaluation tasks. <span id="S2.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lower Left</span>: evaluation pipeline. <span id="S2.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: eight metrics for emotional and role consistencies.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MERRY</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Preliminaries</span></h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>1 </span>Evaluation Task Definition</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the role profile <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and role image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> (for self-localization in videos) of character <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, RPAs step into shoes of this character and engage in multi-turn multimodal emotional dialogues with user named <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, and previous information on current dialogue <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Prev_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Prev_{D}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{RPAs}(Prof_{c},I_{C},N_{u},Prev_{D},D,u_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>RPAs</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{RPAs}(Prof_{c},I_{C},N_{u},Prev_{D},D,u_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\{u_{0},e_{0},...,u_{i-1},e_{i-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\{u_{0},e_{0},...,u_{i-1},e_{i-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a dialogue history sequence, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}=\{A_{i},V_{i},T_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}=\{A_{i},V_{i},T_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th input from user, including audio <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, video <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and content <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=\{f_{i},b_{i},s_{i},c_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=\{f_{i},b_{i},s_{i},c_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th turn response from RPAs, including textual response content <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, facial expression <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, body movement <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and speech prompt <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (namely speech intonation). Notably <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be formatted as JSON, which is most widely used and trained in various base models. <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are the special input and output cases when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>2 </span>Emotion Transition in Dialogue</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Different characters exhibit unique emotional changes during conversations, which can be captured by emotional transition matrices <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Furthermore, a response often comprises multiple utterances separated by punctuation, which may convey different emotions. This suggests that emotional shifts can occur not only inter-turn, but also intra-turn.
Therefore, we further decompose <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the former counts the emotional transitions during each character’s expression process, and the latter counts the emotional changes of the character towards different inputs.
Specifically, given a sample <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with dialogue history <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{u_{0},e_{0},...,u_{i-1},e_{i-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{u_{0},e_{0},...,u_{i-1},e_{i-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, user input <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and target or prediction from system <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the definition of inter-turn emotional transition matrix remains as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, whereas our definition of intra-turn one captures emotion transitions within a single response:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i}TranPair(e_{i-1},e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i}TranPair(e_{i-1},e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i}\sum_{u}^{len^{utts}_{i}}TranPair(e^{i}_{u-1},e^{i}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i}\sum_{u}^{len^{utts}_{i}}TranPair(e^{i}_{u-1},e^{i}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="len^{utts}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">len^{utts}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of utterances obtained by splitting textual response content <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with Chinese punctuation, which also represents the utterance length of the entire multimodal emotional expression <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Pipeline</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Multimodal Role-Playing Datasets ‣ II Related Work ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Lower Left), RC evaluation can directly assess the predicted responses, whereas EC evaluation, requires an additional formatting and emotion recognition pipeline before evaluation.
In this way, the output format is unified despite a broken response prediction, thereby avoiding format bias. Meanwhile, using emotion recognition results from the same group of experts, rather than RPA-generated emotions around response <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, further mitigates model bias.
Specifically, the FORMAT step first attempts to validate the raw output using Pydantic model. If it fails, which is probably due to format issues such as missed quotation marks, Doubao-seed-1-6<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.doubao.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.doubao.com/</a></span></span></span> is invoked to reformat the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="resp^{raw}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">resp^{raw}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> into JSON-formatted output <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="resp^{fmt}_{i}=\{f_{i},b_{i},s_{i},c_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">resp^{fmt}_{i}=\{f_{i},b_{i},s_{i},c_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> without altering its semantics.
When it comes to ERC (Emotional Recognition in Conversation) step, inspired by InstructERC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, five experts are asked to conduct emotion recognition twice for each response <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="len^{utts}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">len^{utts}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> utterances:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{ERC}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{ERC}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=ERC_{exp_{j}}(len^{utts}_{i},resp^{fmt}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=ERC_{exp_{j}}(len^{utts}_{i},resp^{fmt}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> denotes expert index.
The result <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{ERC}_{i,j}=\{emos_{f},emos_{b},emos_{s},emos_{fusion}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{ERC}_{i,j}=\{emos_{f},emos_{b},emos_{s},emos_{fusion}\}</annotation></semantics></math> contains four recognized emotion lists, for facial expression, body movement, speech prompt, and all-modality fusion, respectively. Each emotion list have the same length as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="len^{utts}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">len^{utts}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for instance, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emos_{fusion}=\{emo_{u}\}_{u=1}^{len^{utts}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emos_{fusion}=\{emo_{u}\}_{u=1}^{len^{utts}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Generally, given a formatted response <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="resp^{fmt}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">resp^{fmt}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="len^{utts}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">len^{utts}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> utterances, each modality utterance is associated with ten recognized emotion labels from different experts, forming an emotional distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EmoD_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EmoD_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the emotion label <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emo" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emo</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="prob^{emo}_{utt}\geq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">prob^{emo}_{utt}\geq\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is selected as the final label of the utterance, otherwise it will be marked as <span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“<span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ambiguous</span>”</span> and excluded from the subsequent EC evaluation.
Specifically, in this paper, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is set as 0.7, which means a dominant recognition result agreed by more than two-thirds of the votes. Besides, activated ERC experts are latest models from different series, including Doubao-seed-1-6, GPT-5.2<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://openai.com/api/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://openai.com/api/</a></span></span></span>, Gemini-3-flash<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://gemini.google.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://gemini.google.com/</a></span></span></span>, Claude-sonnet-4.5<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://claude.ai/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://claude.ai/</a></span></span></span>, Deepseek-V3.2<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://deepseek.ai/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://deepseek.ai/</a></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Multimodal Role-Playing Datasets ‣ II Related Work ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Right), we design five metrics for Emotional Consistency and three for Role Consistency.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>1 </span>Emotional Consistency (EC)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EC</span> originally consists of micro Emotional Consistency(EC-micro), Emotional Distribution Divergence(EDD), and Relative Character Discrepancy(RCD), in the context of text conversations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, quantifies how well RPAs can simulate the real and dynamic emotions of designated roles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to measure how precisely and accurately RPAs simulate the real emotions of the designated roles within multimodal dialogues,
<span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">EC-micro</span> should further integrate all multimodal expressions into account, thereby motivating the proposed <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal Emotional Consistency (MEC)</span>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle MEC_{lower|upper}=\frac{\sum_{x=1}^{13}n_{x}\times F1_{x}}{\sum_{x=1}^{13}n_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>13</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>13</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle MEC_{lower|upper}=\frac{\sum_{x=1}^{13}n_{x}\times F1_{x}}{\sum_{x=1}^{13}n_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1},n_{2},...,n_{13}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>13</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1},n_{2},...,n_{13}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F1_{1},F1_{2},...,F1_{13}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mn>13</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F1_{1},F1_{2},...,F1_{13}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of testset samples and their respective F1 scores among the 13 different emotion categories (same as CPED).
Confusion matrix is as shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ III-C1 Emotional Consistency (EC) ‣ III-C Evaluation Metrics ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>,
where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="set()" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">set()</annotation></semantics></math> function is applied because multiple identical emotions might appear in a single response.
Similarly, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC_{lower}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC_{lower}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes matching the exact emotion label, while <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC_{upper}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC_{upper}</annotation></semantics></math> matches the emotional tendency instead.
Both two MEC scores range from 0 to 1, with higher values indicating better performance.
Notably, we selectively omit the original middle value based on Valence-Arousal-Dominance (VAD) emotional vector, since a fixed VAD vector introduces bias for different languages, dialogue context, and roles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emotional Distribution Divergence(EDD)</span> originally measures the realism of dynamic emotional transitions in portrayed characters by comparing them with those observed in real ones,
whereas <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relative Character Discrepancy(RCD)</span> evaluates whether role differences, as reflected by emotional transition matrices, are sufficiently distinguishable yet not overly exaggerated.
Furthermore, as discussed in Section <a href="#S3.SS1.SSS2" title="III-A2 Emotion Transition in Dialogue ‣ III-A Preliminaries ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-A2</span></a>, emotional transition matrices are decomposed into <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, EDD and RCD is also decomposed into <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle EED_{inter|intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle EED_{inter|intra}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{k}^{K}D_{H}(M_{inter|intra}^{GT_{k}}||M_{inter|intra}^{RPA_{k}})}{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{k}^{K}D_{H}(M_{inter|intra}^{GT_{k}}||M_{inter|intra}^{RPA_{k}})}{K}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle CD_{inter|intra}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle CD_{inter|intra}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{role_{A},role_{B}}^{\binom{2}{K}}D_{H}(M_{inter|intra}^{role_{A}}||M_{inter|intra}^{role_{B}})}{\binom{2}{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>2</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{role_{A},role_{B}}^{\binom{2}{K}}D_{H}(M_{inter|intra}^{role_{A}}||M_{inter|intra}^{role_{B}})}{\binom{2}{K}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle RCD_{inter|intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle RCD_{inter|intra}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=CD_{inter|intra}^{RPA}-CD_{inter|intra}^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=CD_{inter|intra}^{RPA}-CD_{inter|intra}^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of roles and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{inter|intra}^{GT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{inter|intra}^{GT}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant value across different RPAs.
Notably, the original unbounded and asymmetric KL distance is replaced as the bounded and symmetric Hellinger distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> (ranging from 0 to 1):</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{H}(P||Q)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i}{(\sqrt{p_{i}}-\sqrt{q_{i}})}^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{H}(P||Q)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\sqrt{\sum_{i}{(\sqrt{p_{i}}-\sqrt{q_{i}})}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore, EED is bounded from 0 to 1, with lower values indicating better performance; whereas RCD is bounded from -1 to 1, with values closer to 0 denoting better alignment with real roles.
Specifically, if RCD <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 0 or increase, it suggests that the model overly emphasize the character differences.
By contrast, this implies that the emotional transitions between roles are too similar, failing to provide sufficient differentiation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>Confusion matrix. <math id="S3.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emo_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emo_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the x-th class emotion. <span id="S3.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GT</span> and <span id="S3.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PD</span> refers to <math id="S3.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emos_{gt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emos_{gt}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emos_{fusion}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emos_{fusion}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. <span id="S3.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“set(list)”</span> function is used to convert the list into unique elements.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T3.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:44.1pt;vertical-align:-19.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(26.8pt,-3.4pt) scale(1.18433553826905,1.18433553826905) ;">
<table id="S3.T3.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T3.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S3.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emo_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emo_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in set(PD)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><math id="S3.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emo_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emo_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> not in set(PD)</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.8.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emo_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emo_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in set(GT)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">TP</td>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">FN</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.8.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S3.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="emo_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">emo_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> not in set(GT)</td>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">FP</td>
<td id="S3.T3.8.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">TN</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">When there are multimodal emotional expressions, EC should further measure the degree of emotional consistency across different modalities, namely <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cross-model Emotional Consistency (CEC)</span>.
Otherwise, inconsistent emotions across modalities will cause a severe sense of dissonance, undermining the immersive experience.
During assessment, Krippendorff’s alpha <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> is applied as metric, since it supports multiple raters and invalid values:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle CEC_{lower|upper}=\alpha_{krippendorff}(\begin{bmatrix}emos_{f}&amp;...\\
emos_{b}&amp;...\\
emos_{s}&amp;...\\
emos_{fusion}&amp;...\end{bmatrix})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle CEC_{lower|upper}=\alpha_{krippendorff}(\begin{bmatrix}emos_{f}&amp;...\\
emos_{b}&amp;...\\
emos_{s}&amp;...\\
emos_{fusion}&amp;...\end{bmatrix})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the four modalities are considered as different raters. Similarly, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CEC_{lower}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CEC_{lower}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CEC_{upper}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CEC_{upper}</annotation></semantics></math> matches exact emotion labels and their tendencies, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we introduce <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emotional Discrepancy (ED)</span> for quantifying how much ambiguity is in the emotion recognition experts, reflecting the precision of emotions conveyed by multimodal expressions to a certain extent:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ED_{fus|f|b|s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ED_{fus|f|b|s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{i}\sum_{u}NormEntropy(EmoD_{u,i}^{fus|f|b|s})}{\sum_{i}len^{utts}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\sum_{i}\sum_{u}NormEntropy(EmoD_{u,i}^{fus|f|b|s})}{\sum_{i}len^{utts}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EmoD_{u,i}^{fus|f|b|s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EmoD_{u,i}^{fus|f|b|s}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the emotional distribution of utterance <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> in sample <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, calculated on different modalities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE IV: </span>Role consistency mapping table.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:86.5pt;vertical-align:-39.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(50.4pt,-12.6pt) scale(1.41280671011203,1.41280671011203) ;">
<table id="S3.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">AgreeFlag</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">0</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">DisagreeFlag</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<table id="S3.T4.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">evidences</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">(agree vs. disagree)</td></tr>
</table></td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">\</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">\</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">&gt;</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">=</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">&lt;</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.3.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">\</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">FinalScore</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">dropped</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">5</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">4</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">3</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">2</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">1</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span>2 </span>Role Consistency (RC)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> is primarily used when evaluating RC.
To improve the consistency between human evaluators and LLMs, we propose to turn the traditional subjective scoring into bidirectional-evidence-finding approach. The final score mapping rule is as exhibited in Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ III-C1 Emotional Consistency (EC) ‣ III-C Evaluation Metrics ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>.
In this way, we improve the consistency and robustness while maintaining the granularity.
Human agreement experimental results in Table <a href="#S4.T10" title="TABLE X ‣ IV-D Effectiveness of MERRY ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">X</span></a> further demonstrate the effectiveness of proposed approach.
During evaluation, we activate two evaluators, including Doubao-seed-1-6 and GPT-5.2. The final score is the average score of two experts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21941v1/data_pipeline.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/394;" width="476" height="394" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span> Construction Pipeline</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Construction of MERRY-Data</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-C2 Role Consistency (RC) ‣ III-C Evaluation Metrics ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we construct <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span>, a multi-turn multimodal role-playing emotional interaction dialogue dataset, based on CPED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Role-related information:</span>
For role profiles and images, we first retrieve role webpage and posters from BaiduBaike<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://baike.baidu.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://baike.baidu.com/</a>, a Chinese Encyclopedia</span></span></span>.
After filtering out missed roles and corresponding dialogues in dataset, we use Deepseek-V3 to summarize into profiles containing four primary parts: basic information, characteristics, experiences, and interpersonal relationships.
For previous information on each dialogue, we retrieve episode information from TVMAO<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.tvmao.com/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.tvmao.com/</a>, a public website including outlines for various Chinese TV episodes</span></span></span>, and use Doubao-seed-1-6 to generate previous information for each dialogue.
Finally we recruit a team of 20 volunteer students from school to check and correct the summarized profiles and role images based on corresponding webpages, and the previous information based on webpages, dialogues, and their episode information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal Detailed Semantic Descriptions:</span>
We propose using detailed semantic descriptions for semantically decoupled training and evaluation, including facial expressions, body movements, and speech prompts (namely intonations), each emotionally distinguishable.
First, we generate coarse-grain semantic descriptions.
For audio modality, we follow current studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> to obtain normalized numerical sequences of <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">speaking speed</span>, <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pitches</span> and <span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">volumes</span> from audios. We then use Deepseek-V3 to analyze those numerical feature sequences and generate speech prompt.
For video modality, we directly use GPT4o-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> to describe facial expressions and body movements based on role images and videos (converted into frames).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we further conduct human-in-the-loop quality control, leveraging the power of cooperation between human and LLMs.
Specifically, we first instruct Doubao-seed-1-6 to separately identify issued descriptions based on video (given frames) and audio (given numerical sequences), and providing suggestions for correction.
Issued descriptions are defined as mismatching (1) videos, (2) emotion labels.
Subsequently, we instruct a team of 20 volunteers to verify these issued descriptions with suggestions.
After two rounds of searching and correction, we acquire a high-quality dataset in the end.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assemble and Statistics:</span>
In this step, we assemble all components above and retain those dialogues with two speakers only.
Finally we divide the dataset into a train set and a test set based on TV series to strictly avoid data leakage.
The statistics are as shown in Tables <a href="#S3.T5" title="TABLE V ‣ III-D Construction of MERRY-Data ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> and <a href="#S3.T6" title="TABLE VI ‣ III-D Construction of MERRY-Data ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE V: </span>Statistics of train and test splits of <span id="S3.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span>.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T5.4" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:276.0pt;height:49.2pt;vertical-align:-21.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(33.7pt,-6.0pt) scale(1.32321538616401,1.32321538616401) ;">
