<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Main Results</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="TABLE VII ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> reports the evaluation results of <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY</span> on different base models with four different training-free role-playing types, including <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All</span>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prof</span> (profile only), <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prev</span> (previous information only), and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span>. We observe that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Across most EC metrics, omni-modal foundation models consistently outperform vision-only counterparts</span>. Among closed-source models, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gemini-2.5-pro</span> is a multimodal foundation model, whereas <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Doubao-1.5v</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPT-5-chat</span> are vision-only. Under the same type condition, regardless of which type, <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gemini-2.5-pro</span> surpasses the other two vision-only models on all sub-metrics of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CEC</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ED" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ED</annotation></semantics></math>, except for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ED_{spe}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ED_{spe}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="type=None" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">type=None</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, the open-source omni-modal foundation model <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MiniCPM-o-2.6(8B)</span> achieves an <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC_{lower}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC_{lower}</annotation></semantics></math> score of 0.264, slightly exceeding those of the two closed-source vision-only models (0.243 and 0.251). This indicates that satisfying omni-modal input conditions is crucial for accurately understanding and consistently expressing characters’ emotions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(2) For RC, all closed-source foundation models exhibit a consistent pattern. Specifically, for the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Exp</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rel" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rel</annotation></semantics></math> metrics, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prev</span> achieves the best performance, followed by <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All</span>, whereas for the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cha</annotation></semantics></math> metric, <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prof</span> performs best, with <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prev</span> as the second best. This pattern suggests that <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Prev<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> provides important information for grounding potentially time-varying knowledge, while </span>Prof<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> appears particularly beneficial for enabling RPAs to exhibit the intended character traits</span></span>.
However, when both types of information are provided simultaneously, namely <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All</span>, performance slightly decreases, which may indicate that longer contextual inputs introduce additional constraints that limit the models’ flexibility in integrating information effectively, leading to a <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+1&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+1&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> effect. By contrast, this pattern is reversed for open-source models. Type <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All</span> almost always yields the best performance (except for the <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cha</annotation></semantics></math> metric of <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-Omni(7B)</span>), exhibiting a <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+1&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+1&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> effect.
This suggests that <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">long-context input may act as a constraint for stronger models, while serving as complementary information for weaker ones, revealing the limitations of current simple prompting methods</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(3) For the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math> metrics, <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All</span> or <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prof</span> consistently achieves the best performance across all tested foundation models. This is because intra-turn dynamic emotional transitions are often highly correlated with character personality traits. Therefore <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">providing profiles helps models express emotions more accurately and role-distinctively</span>, leading to higher <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
In contrast, inter-turn emotional transitions additionally depend on varying inputs and dialogue history, and thus do not exhibit a clear association with any specific role-playing type. Consequently, some models even achieve optimal performance under <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="type=None" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">type=None</annotation></semantics></math>, such as <span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPT-5-chat</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21941v1/case_dynamic_emo.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/224;" width="476" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Emotional transition matrices of Andy in <span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ode to Joy</span>. Groundtruth matrices are list in the first column. The rest columns are role-played by GPT-5-chat with different types.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21941v1/case_MEC_recall_precision_lower.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>The visualization of <span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall</span> and <span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Precision</span> scores of each emotions when calculating <math id="S4.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC_{lower}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC_{lower}</annotation></semantics></math> on all models with <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="type=All" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">type=All</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T8" title="TABLE VIII ‣ IV-A Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a> reports the evaluation results of <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY</span> on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MiniCPM-o-2.6(8B)</span> trained on different role-playing datasets, including <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MMRole</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OmniCharacter</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span> (Ours). We observe that:
<span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">training on synthetic datasets would reduce emotional consistency.</span>
Specifically, <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OmniCharacter</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MMRole</span> are both synthetic multimodal datasets, while <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span> (Ours) are extracted from real multimodal data source.
Model trained on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MMRole</span> exhibits performance drop on <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC_{upper}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC_{upper}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CEC</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD</annotation></semantics></math>, while for <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OmniCharacter</span> the performance drops on <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CEC</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD_{inter}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD_{inter}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD_{intra}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD_{intra}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ED" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ED</annotation></semantics></math>.
The decrease on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OmniCharacter</span> is more severe than that on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MMRole</span>, which can be due to the fixed emotion list in <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OmniCharacter</span>.
By contrast, <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">training on <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY-Data</span> (Ours) could improve emotional consistency</span>, reaching the best performance on <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MEC</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CEC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CEC</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ED" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ED</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, although the performance improves on RC after training, model still potentially struggles with the large number of roles compared to <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MMRole</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OmniCharacter</span>, which contain 85 and 20 roles with a similar or larger sample size, respectively.
Therefore, role-related scores of our trained baseline, involving <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RCD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RCD</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RC" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RC</annotation></semantics></math>, are less than satisfactory, which also aligns with the results of EmoCharacter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, indicating that <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">simple fine-tuning method suffers from poor role generalization</span>.
In summmary, synthetic data is easy to obtain and can be scaled up rapidly, which is helpful to a certain extent for quickly improving RC.
However, the alignment between synthetic and real emotions is relatively poor, which will lead to a decline in EC after training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further explore the reasons and phenomena behind digital experimental results, we conduct in-depth analysis in this section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21941v1/case_RC.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/136;" width="476" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>Average of RC scores of Gemini-2.5-pro based on different episodes of <span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ode to Joy</span>. Notably, <span id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ode to Joy</span> is a 2-series TV. Episodes 1 to 18 are from Series 1, while episodes 19 to 38 are from Series 2.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>1 </span>Why Emotional Inconsistency</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Existing models suffer from emotional templatization and simplification, exhibiting positive-bias and performance bottleneck in fine-grained negative emotions.</span>
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-B Main Results ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, models exhibit a tendency toward the overuse of specific emotions, resulting in high recall but low precision, including <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">happy</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">grateful</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relaxed</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">anger</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sadness</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">worried</span>.
This suggests that existing models have following issues:
(1) <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Positive Biases</span>: After RLHF, models tend to be overly ingratiating, habitually adopting a veneer of gratefulness or happiness regardless of contextual appropriateness;
(2) <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emotional Templatization and Simplification</span>: Common basic emotions such as happiness, anger and sadness are easily adopted by models as a one-size-fits-all template for negative expressions.
This results in misrepresentation of subtle emotions as blunt, template-based ones.
For instance, rendering a state of lacking spirit and motivation (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">depress</span>) as an intense feeling of sorrow and grief (<span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sadness</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, models exhibit restraint in expressing <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">neutral</span> emotion, characterized by high precision but low recall.
This indicates that in multimodal role-playing scenarios, models are easily induced to express emotions with specific tendency, finding it difficult to maintain neutrality.
Finally, categories such as <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">depress</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fear</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">disgust</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">astonished</span> suffer from low recall and low precision simultaneously.
These dimensions represent the current bottlenecks of existing models, reflecting a lack of both the sensitivity and the fidelity required to express complex or visceral human emotions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>2 </span>Why Emotional Transitions Inconsistency</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Existing models struggle with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD</annotation></semantics></math>, which ostensibly measures the consistency of emotional transitions but actually reflecting EC, RC, and human-likeness simultaneously.</span>
As shown in the first column (GT) of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-B Main Results ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Andy tends to shift toward neutral or negative emotions during expression, which is consistent with her personality characterized by rationality, pessimism and realism. This tendency is more pronounced in the intra-turn matrix, further supporting that <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intra-turn dynamic emotional transitions are often more closely related to character personality traits</span>, whereas inter-turn transitions additionally depend on varying contextual information.
In RPAs, because <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prev</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">None</span> do not incorporate profile constraints, they are more inclined to shift toward positive emotions, which leads to lower <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD</annotation></semantics></math> scores.
Although <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All</span>, which includes profile information, is still influenced by the model’s general bias toward positivity, this effect is substantially mitigated, and the emotional transitions gradually align more closely with the ground-truth patterns.
However, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">contrary to the sparse real-world emotion transitions, models potentially tend to exhibit more scattered emotional shifts, resulting in a lower degree of human-likeness</span>.
This also indicates that <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EDD" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EDD</annotation></semantics></math> measures not only the consistencies of roles and emotions, but also the degree of human-likeness, with considerable scope for enhancement across various models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span>3 </span>The Effect of Role-related Information</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV-C Analysis ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, lacking <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prev</span> leads to performance decline on <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Exp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Exp</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Rel" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Rel</annotation></semantics></math> across various episodes, while the lack of <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prof</span> leads to lower <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Cha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Cha</annotation></semantics></math>.
This further demonstrates that <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prev</span> provides important previous information for grounding time-varying knowledge, including experience and relationship, while <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prof</span> is particularly beneficial for exhibiting the intended character traits.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Effectiveness of MERRY</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since, LLMs introduce subjectivity,
we recruit three graduate students to conduct human agreement experiments on:

<span id="S4.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(1)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset Quality</span><span id="S4.I1.i1.3" class="ltx_text">: Human raters are asked to score the semantic consistency between three synthesized descriptions and real-world videos over 100 randomly selected samples in the unchanged subset where the descriptions are identified as <span id="S4.I1.i1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“correct”</span> by LLMs, on a scale of 1 to 5. </span></span>
<span id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(2)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness of LLM-based Metrics</span><span id="S4.I1.i2.3" class="ltx_text">: Human raters are asked to identify bidirectional evidences on three RC metrics with the same instructions over 100 randomly selected samples in evaluation results. Same mapping table as Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ III-C1 Emotional Consistency (EC) ‣ III-C Evaluation Metrics ‣ III MERRY ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> is applied and scores from three raters are averaged, thereby creating groundtruth scores for subsequent comparison.
</span></span>
<span id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(3)</span> <span id="S4.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effectiveness of ERC experts</span><span id="S4.I1.i3.3" class="ltx_text">: Human raters are asked to recognize emotions for each utterance over 100 randomly selected samples in evaluation results, with the same instructions as ERC experts. Notably, two additional human raters are recruited to maintain the same number of experts as <span id="S4.I1.i3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MERRY</span>. Emotions with less than four votes of five human raters are classified as ambiguous emotions.</span></span>
</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE IX: </span>Results of human agreement experiment on description quality. <math id="S4.T9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> refers to Krippendorff’s alpha. <math id="S4.T9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the average score.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T9.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:50pt;vertical-align:-21.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(44.2pt,-6.4pt) scale(1.34411106950422,1.34411106950422) ;">
<table id="S4.T9.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T9.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">facial expression</td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">body movement</td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">speech prompt</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T9.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4.348</td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4.323</td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">4.719</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T9.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.585</td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.539</td>
<td id="S4.T9.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.571</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the scoring results are ordinal data (except for nominal ERC results) evaluated by multiple raters and there are invalid values in RC metrics, we use Krippendorff’s alpha <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> as metric of agreement.
Average scores in Table <a href="#S4.T9" title="TABLE IX ‣ IV-D Effectiveness of MERRY ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IX</span></a> demonstrate substantial semantic consistency (greater than 4) between synthesized descriptions and real videos, with moderate confidence (from 0.4 to 0.6).
Results in Table <a href="#S4.T10" title="TABLE X ‣ IV-D Effectiveness of MERRY ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">X</span></a> also indicate that under the proposed approach, LLM-based metircs have reached to substantial agreement (from 0.6 to 0.8) with human raters on all proposed metrics.
During ablation, the results further demonstrate that depending exclusively on either a single model or subjective scoring approach with explanations can introduce a certain degree of inconsistency.
Finally, results in Table <a href="#S4.T11" title="TABLE XI ‣ IV-D Effectiveness of MERRY ‣ IV Experiments ‣ MERRY: Semantically Decoupled Evaluation of Multimodal Emotional and Role Consistencies of Role-Playing Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XI</span></a> exhibit that human raters and ERC experts have achieved substantial agreement, validating the effectiveness of proposed evaluation setup.
Notably, since role profiles, previous information, and corrected descriptions have already been manually verified during construction, we omit redundant agreement experiments on these contents.</p>
</div>
